  "trivia": [
    {
      "text": "Colossus is entirely computer-generated. Andre Tricoteux stood in for him on set, T. J. Storm recorded his movements again later, and his voice was recast in December 2015 with Stefan Kapičić.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Post-production",
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Visual_effects"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Fox only approved the film after test footage, made in 2012 with a small budget, leaked online in July 2014 to an enthusiastic response. In September 2025 Ryan Reynolds admitted that he had leaked it himself.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Development"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Marvel co-creator Stan Lee has a cameo as the DJ in a strip club.",
      "sources": [
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "text": "The film has about 1,500 visual effects shots, 700 more than planned, and 800 of them were finished in the last four weeks of production.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Visual_effects"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "text": "The film was shot in and around Vancouver from 23 March to 29 May 2015. The freeway fight was filmed on the Georgia Viaduct, which the production had for two weeks, rain or shine.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Filming"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "text": "When Fox would not pay writers Rhett Reese and Paul Wernick to be on set every day, Ryan Reynolds paid their salaries himself.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Filming"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:00:40.000",
      "to": "00:02:26.000",
      "text": "Deadpool co-creator Rob Liefeld is nodded to in the opening titles: a coffee cup with the name \"Rob L.\" flies through the frozen scene. Liefeld also makes a cameo as a mercenary in the bar later in the film.",
      "sources": [
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:00:40.000",
      "to": "00:02:26.000",
      "text": "The opening titles travel through one frozen moment of the fight in the crashing car and credit types instead of names, such as \"Produced by asshats\" and \"Directed by an overpaid tool\". Ryan Reynolds, director Tim Miller and the writers wrote these credits themselves to set the tone of the film.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Visual_effects"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:23:29.080",
      "to": "00:23:56.810",
      "text": "The script set this montage to Frank Sinatra's \"It Was a Very Good Year\". It was changed to Neil Sedaka's \"Calendar Girl\" during editing.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Music"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:30:16.500",
      "to": "00:30:23.200",
      "text": "Deadpool was shot under the working title \"Wham!\". Songs by Wham! and George Michael recur throughout the film, as do references to the Wham! album \"Make It Big\".",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Filming",
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Music"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:37:14.800",
      "to": "00:37:20.000",
      "text": "Negasonic Teenage Warhead was picked from the list of comic characters available to Fox largely for her name, which comes from a 1995 song by the band Monster Magnet.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Writing",
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:40:03.000",
      "to": "00:40:09.300",
      "text": "Deadpool's question whether Professor X is played by James McAvoy or Patrick Stewart \"at this point in the timeline\" was added after filming. Both actors played the character in the X-Men films, and the line became a favourite with many viewers.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Post-production"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "00:41:24.100",
      "to": "00:41:28.000",
      "text": "Asking for a suit that is neither green nor animated is a dig at Green Lantern (2011), in which Ryan Reynolds played the title hero. That film disappointed critics and audiences and set the Deadpool project back.",
      "sources": [
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen",
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Development"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "01:00:01.740",
      "to": "01:01:28.410",
      "text": "The \"Deadpool Rap\" by Teamheadkick plays during the montage of Deadpool hunting for Francis.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Music"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "01:04:51.300",
      "to": "01:04:54.500",
      "text": "As in the comics, Deadpool keeps breaking the fourth wall and talks directly to the audience.",
      "sources": [
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "01:18:20.700",
      "to": "01:18:24.000",
      "text": "Ryan Reynolds improvised this line on set. It was true: there were only two X-Men in the film because of the budget. It became the favourite line of Jim Gianopulos, then chairman of Fox.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Filming"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "01:20:31.300",
      "to": "01:20:40.500",
      "text": "Forty-eight hours before the green light, Fox cut the budget to 58 million dollars. In the rewrite that followed, Deadpool forgets his bag of guns, which saved the production a costly gunfight in the finale.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Writing"
      ],
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "from": "01:46:58.370",
      "to": "01:48:06.240",
      "text": "The post-credits scene parodies the one at the end of Ferris Bueller's Day Off (1986), whose hero also talks to the audience. Deadpool, in a bathrobe, tells everyone to go home and announces Cable for the sequel.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(film)#Writing",
        "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadpool_(Film)#Comicvorlage_und_popkulturelle_Referenzen"
      ],
      "spoiler": true,
      "source": "language-model",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "reviewed": false
    }
  ]
